John maybe we can start with you though  because I'm curious after the film in  2009 when Jim and you felt you had  another story to tell you know we felt  when we were making the first avatar  that if the movie was successful there  were more stories to tell with these  characters set on Pandora to be honest  when the movie was done and we were  finished and we had the incredible  success that Beyond anyone's imagination  um we needed time to digest Jim took two  years and he built a submarine to  explore the Mariana Trench and it was  after that in 2012 that we really sat  down and felt that there was enough  stories to justify making more than one  Sequel and Jim took a year and wrote  1500 pages of story notes and we then  brought on three teams of writers to  create sort of like a writer's room  which is something unusual  and broke down those 1500 pages of notes  into the scripts that that you see now  well I think we can all acknowledge it's  an extraordinary film and we're so glad  that everyone came back for it  so this entire team has worked with Jim  before either on the previous Avatar or  on another one of his films so I'm  curious when you knew this movie was  really going to happen and the following  sequels what you anticipated to be the  most difficult part of your job I  represent  a very large editorial team including  Jim myself John Murphy and our dearly  departed David Brenner  and two additional editors Jason gotio  and Ian silver iron Silverstein and  editorial on an Avatar movie  is unlike editorial on any other movie  and we are there from the very beginning  when performances are captured  we put together performance edits that  are approved and meticulously selected  performances  and those shots and scenes are prepared  for virtual photography which create  the template for each scene and in this  particular film with the enormous amount  of live-action integration into virtual  character scenes  we had to capture a placeholder for  every live-action character  that would ultimately be replaced with  live action photography  so we had many stages of editing  throughout this process over the years  and it is it is a very complicated  process  um Russell I'm curious for you when you  came on especially with the water part  of this story how did you anticipate  that would make your job harder  well I've had a little experience with  water before with it with Jim and I knew  it was going to be difficult and and  that part was uh I came into it  basically though I came under this show  as a as a newcomer and basically I  thought well here we have a there's  already a template for what Pandora  looks like and when I talked with Jim  about how we were going to expand on  this he says well we have a lot of new  worlds here to look at and we talked  about the very very nuanced light that  you find in the forces Forest of Pandora  Under The Canopy uh nothing the light  was going to be living  breathing as much as the planet did that  uh there were going to be very much uh  very many different colors uh and that  canopy and then we have a world of the  RDA which is its own its own language of  of light so basically I started off by  lighting some scenes uh supervising some  scenes with the lighting and uh uh I'm  very happy with the way those went uh  while I was doing that uh I was uh  prepping cameras there was uh there was  so much new technology and then I also  had to speak the language of virtual too  and there there was this uh this wedding  of uh the live action photography and  the virtual teams and uh we had a few  bumps but eventually it went very well  and we were very supportive of each  other and I just want to clarify Russ  when you talk about the lighting in a  year of lighting we brought Russ on a  year before we got to our live action  filming because we wanted him to be a  part of the lighting style of what we  call the virtual world the pandoran  world so Russell you know is is the  auteur who who came in and gave his  sensibility for the lighting because we  needed to match it in the live action so  that's why when he refers to that yeah  so Dylan tell me about creating the  world of the Met kaina because I think  it's so unique but feels authentic to  what that culture if it existed would  look like yeah creating that world was  Far and Away the the biggest challenge  on on my side of things for for  production design and real quick I just  want to explain we have two production  designers myself and Ben Proctor and  it's divided along the lines of I  basically am in charge of all things  Pandora  um environments you know cultural  creatures all that stuff and Ben is in  charge of everything human it's all the  RDA you know human sets and vehicles and  all that so on my side the the mechanic  Village is Far and Away the most  difficult Jim had a basic idea of these  large mangroves and there would be a  village suspended underneath it  and beyond that it was up to us to  figure it out you know like Jim has a  has a has a vision but trying to realize  that and bring that into reality was Far  and Away the biggest challenge and so we  did many types of Explorations we did a  lot of practical Explorations where we  would build tensile models even going so  far as to engage what a workshop into  building a large-scale miniature of of  the Salim rui the the the the Sully's  home at metcaina where  um we engaged the best Weavers in New  Zealand and we wove a home and bent came  and did this for real and figured this  stuff out for real  um you know even after rigor's design  process in Los Angeles with  illustrations and 3D models still didn't  like okay that's great let's test this  and let's do this for real and weave it  and  um  while that was not a shooting miniature  it became the Practical reference and  the standard of photorealism that that  the visual effects had to be held up to  and that and some other walkway studies  informed the entire Village and so um in  Avatar one there wasn't a big  architectural Focus  um they lived in hometry and there was  some you know some props and some  hammocks and a few things but this was  getting way more into knobby design and  we see how the mechania are much more  advanced Weavers  um you know and it was very important is  to show cultural differences and to show  that there is diversity on this large  Moon we think of it a plant but it's a  moon and that um it was important to  separate those those cultures and to see  how they draw inspiration from the sea  and that that is part of their life you  know and that connectivity is a huge  huge theme for us so the whole walkway  system is all tensile structured  literally connecting everything there  just as they are connected to the ocean  just how in the oceans we you know  connect it with the fractal core oral  shapes and all the sinuous shapes and  the roots of the spirit tree so we're  really  trying to find that diversity but then  also finding the common themes of  connectivity which is you know one of  the major things we see in Avatar and  Deborah what did Jim ask of you on this  one that you had to deliver oh my  goodness Jim has such a vivid and  immense imagination which is at once out  of a huge like oh my God what a task  that will be but he also just opens the  door to every creative bone in your body  so we're so fortunate to have him as our  leader and inspiration really  um so you know it was it was a big  project I was the costume designer for  Live Action and for the virtual world I  did we did all the performance capture  we did a you know we had to shoot  reference upon reference upon reference  but most importantly I think Jim's most  important  thing to me was we were going to make  every single one of those virtual  costumes that that the way they were  made how they were made how they were  worn was to inform the digital and VFX  team so  it was daunting and it was marvelous it  was absolutely the best fun I've ever  had  how did you first approach the score I  mean it sounds like authenticity was  such a key part of a lot of these  conversations was that the same for you  yes I think Jim asked me to read all of  the scripts in December 2017. and on the  first paragraph of two it says naturi  sings the song chord which is this and  this was a thread that wove itself  through the film and so I had this  immediate thing that said to me music is  important to this film and it has a  reason to exist so my first task was to  write the song chord for Nigeria to sing  um and I looked at that and one of the  things we were working in a Thing Called  Culture Club which was the idea that Jim  said you just can't make stuff up I  wanted to write this in in Navi not in  English it had to feel grounded in  Pandora so I started looking around the  world at various different uh indigenous  Customs to do with birth and death and  so on and there's one that resonated  with me with this and it occurs all over  the world is the idea of a spark  being starting and then the dawn coming  and you carry the light with you through  the day and then eventually you go to  the Sunset and I use that as my sort of  home for the lyric I then had to give  this lyric to Jim I wrote it in English  first I said Tim this is where I want to  be and then I translated it roughly into  Navi and then worked with Paul from the  chief linguist and designer of the navi  language once I had the song chord I  started thinking about other themes that  were to go through the film because you  know there are obviously there are the  family is the core of the music in this  and you can see that in the way that the  song chord then expanded into a theme as  they're leaving home tree you actually  hear the song chord but it's a big  orchestral theme  but there's also there is the Sea and  the mechanina and their connection  um and that became a theme that I used  um for when they're swimming underwater  the first time and when the tolkun  arrived and I had one other massive  responsibility in some ways in that  because so much of this happens  underwater  there isn't any dialogue and the sound  effects are so relatively minimal so in  some ways sometimes I had to be that  extra character the music had to act as  an extra character within the film and  so there were certain scenes where Jim  would say to me we have to do this again  you know we would redo these things many  times There is five hours of finished  score music has always meant something  to a Jim Cameron film and I think this  was one of those times where he he  challenged me he pushed me and I think  um you know that was part of what you  know it had to be woven into the film so  13 years since the last film came out  what did time allow you in making this  film successful what was it it's the  best gift having this much time gave to  you time allowed us to present what you  saw today  I don't think we could have done this  five years ago I don't think we could  have done this and I I think that you  know  Cinema  that word Cinema  we all have to fight to preserve it in  in today's world and that was really one  of our goals was to create something  when when audiences around the world go  to see Avatar the way of water they are  reminded of just how special the movie  going experience can be one of the lines  Jim wrote on the first avatar it's in  this one again enate kamea I see you and  each movie  is designed about seeing things and  seeing people for who they are and  appreciating that  well thank you so much for joining us  please give him another round of event  thank you very much

